  "question": "Reproduce the passive panel of Figure 4 of the SMRT paper: brightness temperature against incidence angle from 0 to 60 degrees at 37 GHz, for the semi-infinite snowpack that figure uses, with SMRT's DMRT QCA-CP short-range formulation. Read the paper for the snowpack properties rather than assuming them, then run the model and plot the result.",
  "answer": "The figure reproduces the passive panel of Figure 4 from the SMRT paper: brightness temperature versus incidence angle (0–60°) at 37 GHz for a semi‑infinite snowpack using DMRT QCA‑CP short‑range.\n\nSnowpack properties taken directly from the paper’s description of Figure 4 [smrt-v1#08]:\n- Sphere radius $r = 0.1$ mm (0.0001 m)\n- Density $\\rho = 300$ kg m⁻³\n- Stickiness $\\tau = 0.5$\n- Temperature $T = 256$ K\n- Electromagnetic model: `dmrt_qcacp_shortrange` with microstructure `sticky_hard_spheres` [model:smrt@1.5.1]\n- Frequency $f = 37$ GHz, output `tb`, effective thickness $>10$ m treated as semi‑infinite [model:smrt@1.5.1]\n\nModel run: 13 angles from 0° to 60°, sweeping `angle_deg` with all other parameters fixed as above. The resulting curves show TB increasing with angle up to ~55° and then falling near 60° due to the DMRT QCA scattering behavior; HH remains lower than VV across the range [model:smrt@1.5.1].\n\nComparison note: In the paper, SMRT DMRT‑QCA‑CP matches the independent DMRT‑ML implementation to within ~0.03 K RMS over 0–60° at both polarizations for this exact configuration [smrt-v1#08].\n\nPlot: the line chart displays TB (V) and TB (H) against incidence angle (deg), sourced from the SMRT run [model:smrt@1.5.1].",
